const courses = [{"id":"biology","title":"1. Biology for Neuroscience","blurb":"Build the biology foundation behind every later brain topic.","level":"Foundation","time":"18 min","sections":[{"title":"What makes a living cell?","content":[["p","Cells are the basic units of living organisms. A cell has a boundary, internal machinery, genetic information, and systems that capture and use energy."],["p","Neural tissue follows these same rules. Neurons and glia are specialized cells, but they still depend on membranes, proteins, energy production, and communication."]],"terms":["cell","homeostasis","metabolism"]},{"title":"Proteins do the work","content":[["p","Proteins can act as enzymes, receptors, channels, transporters, structural components, and signals. The shape of a protein helps determine what it can do."],["p","In neuroscience, protein function becomes especially important when you learn about ion channels, neurotransmitter receptors, molecular motors, and synaptic proteins."]],"terms":["protein","enzyme","receptor","ion channel"]},{"title":"Genetic information","content":[["p","DNA stores genetic information. Genes provide instructions for making functional products, often through the production of RNA and proteins."],["p","Brain cells use the same genetic code as other cells, but different cells turn different genes on or off. That helps produce different cell types and functions."]],"terms":["DNA","gene","RNA","gene expression"]},{"title":"Homeostasis and the brain","content":[["p","Homeostasis means keeping internal conditions within useful ranges. The nervous system both depends on homeostasis and helps control it."],["p","Temperature, blood chemistry, glucose availability, and other variables affect brain function. Maintaining stable conditions is a major biological requirement."]],"terms":["homeostasis","internal environment","regulation"],"quiz":{"question":"What is the basic unit of living organisms?","options":["Cell","Organ","Bone","Synapse"],"correct":0,"why":"Cells are the basic units of living organisms."}}]},{"id":"cells","title":"2. Cells, Tissues & Organelles","blurb":"Zoom inside cells and learn the structures that keep neural tissue alive.","level":"Foundation","time":"20 min","sections":[{"title":"The cell membrane","content":[["p","The cell membrane is a flexible boundary made mainly from a lipid bilayer with embedded proteins. It separates the inside of the cell from the outside environment."],["p","Membrane proteins allow controlled communication and transport. In neurons, this selective boundary is essential for electrical signaling."]],"terms":["cell membrane","lipid bilayer","membrane protein"]},{"title":"Mitochondria and cellular energy","content":[["p","Mitochondria help produce ATP, a major energy currency of cells. Neurons use large amounts of energy to maintain ion gradients, move materials, and support synapses."],["p","Energy demand is one reason nervous tissue is sensitive to prolonged interruptions in oxygen and nutrient delivery."]],"terms":["mitochondria","ATP","cellular respiration"]},{"title":"Nucleus, ribosomes and protein production","content":[["p","The nucleus contains most of a cell’s DNA. Ribosomes build proteins using information carried by RNA."],["p","Neurons constantly make proteins for receptors, channels, membranes, and cellular maintenance. Protein production continues throughout a neuron’s life."]],"terms":["nucleus","ribosome","RNA"]},{"title":"Tissues and specialized cells","content":[["p","A tissue is a group of cells organized for related functions. Nervous tissue contains neurons plus several types of glial cells."],["p","Specialization lets different cells divide the work. The nervous system works because these cells interact rather than because any one cell does everything."]],"terms":["tissue","neural tissue","specialization"],"quiz":{"question":"Which molecule is commonly described as a major cellular energy currency?","options":["ATP","DNA","Myelin","Keratin"],"correct":0,"why":"ATP is a major energy currency used by cells."}}]},{"id":"anatomy-language","title":"3. Anatomy Language","blurb":"Learn the map words used to describe where structures sit in the body and brain.","level":"Foundation","time":"16 min","sections":[{"title":"Anatomical position","content":[["p","Anatomical descriptions use a standard reference position so that body directions remain consistent. This matters even when the person is lying down or turned around."],["p","Precise vocabulary prevents mistakes when two structures are close together."]],"terms":["anatomical position","orientation"]},{"title":"Directional terms","content":[["p","Superior means toward the head and inferior means toward the feet. Anterior means toward the front and posterior means toward the back."],["p","Medial means closer to the middle line of the body, while lateral means farther from it. Proximal and distal describe relative distance from a point of attachment."]],"terms":["superior","inferior","anterior","posterior","medial","lateral","proximal","distal"]},{"title":"Planes and sections","content":[["p","Sagittal sections divide left and right. Coronal sections divide front and back. Axial sections divide upper and lower portions."],["p","Medical images can be displayed in different planes. Learning to imagine the three dimensional structure behind a two dimensional image is an important skill."]],"terms":["sagittal","coronal","axial","section"]},{"title":"Brain orientation is special","content":[["p","In the brain, words such as rostral and caudal are often used to describe direction. Many brain structures are also described using medial and lateral relationships."],["p","The brain develops from a curved tube, so its orientation is not always as intuitive as the rest of the body. Diagrams and repeated practice help."]],"terms":["rostral","caudal","neuroanatomy"],"quiz":{"question":"Which term means toward the back?","options":["Anterior","Posterior","Medial","Superior"],"correct":1,"why":"Posterior describes a direction toward the back."}}]},{"id":"neuron-anatomy","title":"4. Neuron Anatomy","blurb":"Trace the parts of a neuron and connect structure with function.","level":"Core","time":"18 min","sections":[{"title":"Dendrites and inputs","content":[["p","Dendrites are branching neuronal processes that commonly receive incoming signals from other cells."],["p","Many dendrites contain specialized structures called spines. Dendritic shape and branching influence how a neuron receives information."]],"terms":["dendrite","dendritic spine","input"]},{"title":"The soma","content":[["p","The soma, or cell body, contains the nucleus and much of the machinery that keeps the neuron alive."],["p","It also participates in integrating incoming signals with the neuron’s overall electrical state."]],"terms":["soma","cell body","nucleus"]},{"title":"Axons and terminals","content":[["p","The axon is a neuronal process that commonly carries electrical signals away from the cell body toward target cells."],["p","At axon terminals, the neuron can communicate across synapses. Some axons are very short, while others extend long distances."]],"terms":["axon","axon terminal","target cell"]},{"title":"The axon initial segment","content":[["p","The axon initial segment sits near the beginning of the axon. In many neurons it is a key region for action potential initiation."],["p","This makes it an important link between small incoming voltage changes and the large regenerative signal of an action potential."]],"terms":["axon initial segment","action potential"],"quiz":{"question":"Which neuronal region is a common site of action potential initiation?","options":["Axon initial segment","Nucleus","Dendrite only","Meninges"],"correct":0,"why":"The axon initial segment is a common site of action potential initiation in many neurons."}}]},{"id":"glia","title":"5. Glia & Myelin","blurb":"Meet the support cells that keep neural circuits working.","level":"Core","time":"18 min","sections":[{"title":"Why glia matter","content":[["p","Glial cells are active participants in nervous system function. They help maintain the environment around neurons, support signaling, form insulation, and participate in defense and repair."],["p","The word glia describes a diverse group of cell types rather than one single kind of cell."]],"terms":["glia","neuroglia","support"]},{"title":"Astrocytes","content":[["p","Astrocytes help regulate the local chemical environment around neurons and contribute to relationships between neural tissue and blood vessels."],["p","They also communicate with other cells and respond to changes in nervous tissue."]],"terms":["astrocyte","extracellular environment"]},{"title":"Oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells","content":[["p","Oligodendrocytes form myelin in the central nervous system. Schwann cells form myelin in the peripheral nervous system."],["p","Myelin helps electrically insulate axons and allows rapid signaling along many nerve fibers."]],"terms":["oligodendrocyte","Schwann cell","myelin"]},{"title":"Microglia","content":[["p","Microglia are immune related cells of the central nervous system. They can survey the local environment and respond to injury or infection."],["p","Understanding microglia becomes important when learning about inflammation, neurodegeneration, and repair."]],"terms":["microglia","neuroinflammation"],"quiz":{"question":"Which cells form myelin in the central nervous system?","options":["Oligodendrocytes","Schwann cells","Red blood cells","Platelets"],"correct":0,"why":"Oligodendrocytes form myelin in the central nervous system."}}]},{"id":"resting","title":"6. Resting Membrane Potential","blurb":"Understand why a resting neuron has a voltage difference across its membrane.","level":"Core","time":"20 min","sections":[{"title":"Voltage across a membrane","content":[["p","A neuron at rest has a membrane potential, meaning the electrical potential inside the cell differs from that outside it."],["p","A common textbook example is about negative 70 millivolts inside relative to outside, but real neurons can have different resting values."]],"terms":["membrane potential","resting potential"]},{"title":"Ion gradients","content":[["p","Ions are distributed unevenly across the membrane. Sodium is more concentrated outside many neurons, while potassium is more concentrated inside."],["p","These concentration differences represent stored electrochemical energy that can influence membrane voltage when channels open."]],"terms":["ion gradient","sodium","potassium","electrochemical gradient"]},{"title":"Selective permeability","content":[["p","The membrane is not equally permeable to every ion. At rest, potassium leak pathways make an important contribution to the membrane potential."],["p","The resting potential is therefore the result of several interacting factors rather than one single switch."]],"terms":["selective permeability","potassium leak","channel"]},{"title":"The sodium potassium pump","content":[["p","The sodium potassium pump uses ATP to move sodium and potassium against their concentration gradients. This helps maintain the gradients over time."],["p","The pump is essential for long term maintenance, while selective membrane permeability has a major role in the immediate resting voltage."]],"terms":["sodium potassium pump","ATP","gradient maintenance"],"quiz":{"question":"Which ion commonly has a major influence on the resting potential through leak pathways?","options":["Potassium","DNA","Iron","Cholesterol"],"correct":0,"why":"Potassium leak conductance is an important contributor to resting membrane potential."}}]},{"id":"action","title":"7. Action Potentials","blurb":"Follow the electrical sequence that lets neurons send rapid signals.","level":"Core","time":"24 min","sections":[{"title":"Threshold and the all or none idea","content":[["p","An action potential is a rapid change in membrane voltage. When membrane conditions reach the relevant threshold, voltage gated channels can produce a regenerative response."],["p","For a single neuron at a given site, an action potential is often described as all or none: it occurs fully or does not occur, rather than getting a little bit stronger with a stronger stimulus."]],"terms":["threshold","all or none","voltage gated channel"]},{"title":"Depolarization","content":[["p","During the rising phase of a typical action potential, voltage gated sodium channels open and sodium moves inward."],["p","The inward sodium current makes the membrane potential more positive, which opens additional sodium channels and creates a rapid positive feedback cycle."]],"terms":["depolarization","Na plus","positive feedback"]},{"title":"Repolarization","content":[["p","Voltage gated sodium channels become inactivated, while potassium channels contribute strongly to the falling phase."],["p","Potassium moving outward helps bring the membrane potential back toward negative values."]],"terms":["repolarization","Na channel inactivation","K plus"]},{"title":"Hyperpolarization and refractory periods","content":[["p","Potassium conductance can remain elevated briefly, causing the membrane to become more negative than its resting level. This is called hyperpolarization."],["p","Refractory periods help limit how quickly another action potential can be generated and help support one way propagation along an axon."]],"terms":["hyperpolarization","refractory period"],"quiz":{"question":"Which ion is especially important during the rising phase of a typical neuronal action potential?","options":["Sodium","Potassium","Calcium in the nucleus","Chloride only"],"correct":0,"why":"Voltage gated sodium channels open during the rising phase and sodium moves inward."}}],"lab":true},{"id":"synapses","title":"8. Synapses & Neurotransmitters","blurb":"Learn how neurons communicate across chemical and electrical synapses.","level":"Core","time":"22 min","sections":[{"title":"What is a synapse?","content":[["p","A synapse is a communication junction between a neuron and another cell. In a chemical synapse, the communicating cells are separated by a tiny extracellular space."],["p","Synapses let nervous systems combine information, modify circuit strength, and control many targets."]],"terms":["synapse","presynaptic","postsynaptic"]},{"title":"Vesicles and release","content":[["p","When an action potential reaches many presynaptic terminals, calcium enters through voltage gated calcium channels. This calcium helps trigger neurotransmitter release from vesicles."],["p","The released neurotransmitter crosses the synaptic cleft and binds to receptors on the target cell."]],"terms":["synaptic vesicle","calcium","synaptic cleft"]},{"title":"Ionotropic and metabotropic receptors","content":[["p","Ionotropic receptors are ligand gated ion channels that can change membrane currents quickly. Metabotropic receptors act through signaling pathways and often have slower, more modulatory effects."],["p","Both types matter. Fast signaling and slower modulation can work together in the same circuit."]],"terms":["ionotropic","metabotropic","receptor"]},{"title":"Excitation and inhibition","content":[["p","Excitatory and inhibitory effects depend on the receptors and ion gradients in the receiving cell. A neurotransmitter is not automatically excitatory or inhibitory in every situation."],["p","The brain computes through combinations of signals, not one neurotransmitter acting alone."]],"terms":["excitation","inhibition","receptor context"],"quiz":{"question":"What ion is a key trigger for neurotransmitter release at many chemical synapses?","options":["Calcium","Iron","Potassium from the nucleus","DNA"],"correct":0,"why":"Calcium entry into the presynaptic terminal helps trigger vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter release."}}]},{"id":"spinal","title":"9. Spinal Cord & Reflexes","blurb":"Map the spinal cord, gray and white matter, roots, and simple reflex circuits.","level":"Core","time":"22 min","sections":[{"title":"The spinal cord map","content":[["p","The spinal cord connects the brain with the body and contains circuits that can process information locally."],["p","It is organized into regions that correspond broadly with cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral levels, with additional coccygeal anatomy."]],"terms":["spinal cord","cervical","thoracic","lumbar","sacral"]},{"title":"Gray matter and white matter","content":[["p","Gray matter contains many neuronal cell bodies, dendrites, and synapses. White matter contains many myelinated axons traveling through the nervous system."],["p","The pattern of gray and white matter differs between the spinal cord and brain, so orientation matters when interpreting anatomy."]],"terms":["gray matter","white matter","tract"]},{"title":"Dorsal and ventral roots","content":[["p","Dorsal roots carry sensory information into the spinal cord, while ventral roots carry motor information out toward muscles and other targets."],["p","Spinal nerves form from the joining of these roots, creating mixed nerves that can contain both sensory and motor fibers."]],"terms":["dorsal root","ventral root","spinal nerve"]},{"title":"Reflex arcs","content":[["p","A reflex can be organized as a pathway from a sensory receptor to a sensory neuron, spinal circuit, motor neuron, and target tissue."],["p","Reflexes can happen without waiting for the brain to plan every detail. The brain still receives information about many reflex events."]],"terms":["reflex arc","sensory receptor","motor neuron"],"quiz":{"question":"Which roots carry sensory information into the spinal cord?","options":["Dorsal roots","Ventral roots","Optic nerves","Cranial sutures"],"correct":0,"why":"Dorsal roots carry sensory information into the spinal cord."}}]},{"id":"cranial","title":"10. Cranial Nerves","blurb":"Learn the twelve cranial nerves and the jobs they perform.","level":"Core","time":"24 min","sections":[{"title":"The twelve nerve map","content":[["p","Twelve cranial nerves are traditionally numbered I through XII. They carry sensory information, motor commands, or both, and several participate in autonomic functions."],["p","Learning the names first is useful, but learning the function and location is more important than memorizing a list without context."]],"terms":["cranial nerves","CN I","CN XII"]},{"title":"Sensory cranial nerves","content":[["p","The olfactory nerve is associated with smell, and the optic nerve with vision. The vestibulocochlear nerve carries information related to hearing and balance."],["p","These nerves connect specialized sensory systems with the brain."]],"terms":["olfactory","optic","vestibulocochlear"]},{"title":"Motor cranial nerves","content":[["p","The oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nerves control different eye movements. The hypoglossal nerve controls many tongue movements."],["p","Other cranial nerves also carry motor fibers, so classification is often mixed rather than purely sensory or purely motor."]],"terms":["oculomotor","trochlear","abducens","hypoglossal"]},{"title":"The vagus nerve","content":[["p","The vagus nerve is a major cranial nerve with widespread connections to structures in the head, neck, thorax, and abdomen."],["p","It carries both sensory and motor information and has important parasympathetic roles."]],"terms":["vagus nerve","parasympathetic"],"quiz":{"question":"Which cranial nerve is strongly associated with hearing and balance?","options":["Vestibulocochlear nerve","Optic nerve","Olfactory nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"correct":0,"why":"The vestibulocochlear nerve carries information related to hearing and balance."}}]},{"id":"brain-anatomy","title":"11. Major Brain Anatomy","blurb":"Build the large scale map of the cerebrum, cerebellum, brainstem, and diencephalon.","level":"Core","time":"22 min","sections":[{"title":"The cerebrum","content":[["p","The cerebrum is the largest major division of the human brain. Its two cerebral hemispheres contain extensive cortical and subcortical structures."],["p","Many functions such as perception, voluntary movement, language, and complex planning depend on networks within the cerebrum."]],"terms":["cerebrum","hemisphere","cortex"]},{"title":"The cerebellum","content":[["p","The cerebellum helps coordinate movement, timing, balance, posture, and motor learning."],["p","It contains a highly organized cortex and many internal nuclei that interact with the brainstem and cerebrum."]],"terms":["cerebellum","motor learning","coordination"]},{"title":"The brainstem","content":[["p","The brainstem includes the midbrain, pons, and medulla. It contains important pathways, cranial nerve nuclei, and circuits involved in arousal and autonomic functions."],["p","It also forms a major connection between the brain, spinal cord, and cerebellum."]],"terms":["midbrain","pons","medulla","brainstem"]},{"title":"The diencephalon","content":[["p","The diencephalon includes structures such as the thalamus and hypothalamus. It sits deep within the brain and connects broad systems."],["p","Its nuclei participate in sensory processing, movement, homeostasis, attention, and endocrine regulation."]],"terms":["diencephalon","thalamus","hypothalamus"],"quiz":{"question":"Which structure is part of the brainstem?","options":["Pons","Hippocampus","Corpus callosum","Thalamus"],"correct":0,"why":"The pons is one of the three major parts of the brainstem."}}]},{"id":"cortex","title":"12. Cerebral Cortex","blurb":"Study the layered cortex, major regions, and how networks create functions.","level":"Core","time":"24 min","sections":[{"title":"What is the cortex?","content":[["p","The cerebral cortex is the outer layer of gray matter covering the cerebral hemispheres. It contains many neurons organized into layers and interconnected networks."],["p","Functions are distributed across networks. A single named region can be important without being solely responsible for a complex behavior."]],"terms":["cerebral cortex","gray matter","cortical layer"]},{"title":"Frontal lobe","content":[["p","The frontal lobe contains regions involved in voluntary movement, planning, decision making, working memory, and aspects of language and behavior."],["p","The precentral gyrus contains primary motor cortex, which is arranged in a map of the body."]],"terms":["frontal lobe","precentral gyrus","motor cortex"]},{"title":"Parietal, temporal and occipital lobes","content":[["p","The parietal lobe participates in body sensation and spatial processing. The temporal lobe contributes to hearing, memory, language, and recognition."],["p","The occipital lobe is strongly involved in visual processing. These broad labels are starting points for a more detailed map."]],"terms":["parietal lobe","temporal lobe","occipital lobe"]},{"title":"Cortical maps","content":[["p","Primary sensory and motor areas are organized into maps. Neighboring regions often correspond to neighboring parts of the body or features of sensory space."],["p","The maps are not fixed diagrams carved into the brain. They can change with development, learning, and injury."]],"terms":["somatotopy","cortical map","primary cortex"],"quiz":{"question":"Which lobe contains the primary visual cortex?","options":["Occipital","Frontal","Parietal","Temporal"],"correct":0,"why":"Primary visual cortex is located in the occipital lobe."}}]},{"id":"deep","title":"13. Deep Brain Structures","blurb":"Explore the thalamus, hypothalamus, basal ganglia, hippocampus, amygdala, and related circuits.","level":"Core","time":"26 min","sections":[{"title":"Thalamus","content":[["p","The thalamus contains many nuclei that receive, process, and relay information to the cerebral cortex. It participates in sensory processing, motor circuits, attention, and more."],["p","It is better to think of the thalamus as a collection of specialized nuclei than as one single relay station."]],"terms":["thalamus","nucleus","relay"]},{"title":"Hypothalamus","content":[["p","The hypothalamus helps maintain homeostasis and links neural regulation with endocrine and autonomic functions."],["p","It participates in temperature regulation, hunger, thirst, circadian rhythms, stress responses, and reproductive physiology."]],"terms":["hypothalamus","homeostasis","endocrine","autonomic"]},{"title":"Basal ganglia","content":[["p","The basal ganglia are a set of deep brain structures involved in movement selection, initiation, learning, and habits."],["p","Important structures include the caudate, putamen, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, and substantia nigra. Their effects depend on interacting pathways."]],"terms":["basal ganglia","caudate","putamen","substantia nigra"]},{"title":"Hippocampus and amygdala","content":[["p","The hippocampus is important for forming and organizing certain kinds of memories. The amygdala participates in emotional processing, threat learning, and assigning significance to experiences."],["p","These structures work with larger networks rather than acting as isolated memory or emotion boxes."]],"terms":["hippocampus","amygdala","memory","emotion"],"quiz":{"question":"Which structure is strongly involved in memory formation?","options":["Hippocampus","Medulla","Optic nerve","Spinal disc"],"correct":0,"why":"The hippocampus is important for forming and organizing certain memories."}}]},{"id":"brainstem-cerebellum","title":"14. Brainstem & Cerebellum","blurb":"Go deeper into pathways, cranial nerve nuclei, arousal, coordination, and balance.","level":"Core","time":"24 min","sections":[{"title":"Midbrain","content":[["p","The midbrain contains pathways and nuclei involved in eye movements, movement control, sensory processing, and arousal."],["p","It also contains structures that interact with the basal ganglia and spinal cord."]],"terms":["midbrain","tectum","substantia nigra"]},{"title":"Pons","content":[["p","The pons connects multiple parts of the nervous system and contains pathways and cranial nerve related nuclei."],["p","It is especially important in communication between the cerebrum and cerebellum and in several brainstem functions."]],"terms":["pons","cerebellum","cranial nerve nuclei"]},{"title":"Medulla","content":[["p","The medulla contains important pathways and autonomic circuits. It participates in the control of breathing, cardiovascular functions, and protective reflexes."],["p","Because critical functions pass through or are controlled by brainstem structures, brainstem anatomy is central to neurology and neurosurgery."]],"terms":["medulla","autonomic","brainstem reflex"]},{"title":"Cerebellar circuits","content":[["p","The cerebellum compares information about intended and ongoing movement and helps refine timing and coordination."],["p","It also contributes to motor learning. Its connections are organized through cerebellar peduncles that link it with the brainstem."]],"terms":["cerebellar cortex","peduncle","motor learning"],"quiz":{"question":"Which brain structure is especially important for coordination and motor learning?","options":["Cerebellum","Thalamus only","Meninges","Pituitary gland"],"correct":0,"why":"The cerebellum is strongly involved in coordination, timing, balance, and motor learning."}}]},{"id":"pathways","title":"15. Sensory & Motor Pathways","blurb":"Trace information from body receptors to the brain and from the brain to movement.","level":"Applied","time":"28 min","sections":[{"title":"Sensory receptors","content":[["p","Sensory receptors detect changes such as touch, temperature, stretch, light, sound, or tissue damage."],["p","Receptors convert physical or chemical changes into signals that can be carried by sensory neurons."]],"terms":["sensory receptor","transduction","afferent"]},{"title":"Ascending pathways","content":[["p","Sensory information travels through pathways that carry signals upward toward the brain. Different modalities use different pathways with specific crossings and relay points."],["p","Learning these pathways means tracking three things: where the signal starts, where it crosses, and where it ends."]],"terms":["ascending tract","decussation","relay"]},{"title":"Motor pathways","content":[["p","Voluntary movement depends on networks that include the motor cortex, brainstem pathways, spinal cord circuits, and peripheral motor neurons."],["p","The corticospinal tract is a major descending pathway for voluntary motor control."]],"terms":["motor cortex","corticospinal tract","descending pathway"]},{"title":"Upper and lower motor neurons","content":[["p","Upper motor neurons are neurons in the central nervous system whose pathways influence lower motor neurons. Lower motor neurons directly innervate skeletal muscle."],["p","The distinction helps organize neurological examination findings and lesion localization."]],"terms":["upper motor neuron","lower motor neuron","skeletal muscle"],"quiz":{"question":"Which tract is a major descending pathway for voluntary motor control?","options":["Corticospinal tract","Optic tract","Spinothalamic tract only","Olfactory tract"],"correct":0,"why":"The corticospinal tract is a major descending pathway for voluntary movement."}}]},{"id":"blood","title":"16. Brain Blood Supply","blurb":"Learn the major arterial territories, venous drainage, and why brain blood flow matters.","level":"Applied","time":"26 min","sections":[{"title":"Why the brain needs blood flow","content":[["p","Brain tissue depends on a continuous supply of oxygen and glucose. Blood flow delivers these resources and helps remove metabolic waste."],["p","Neurons have limited energy reserves, so interruptions in circulation can rapidly disturb function."]],"terms":["cerebral blood flow","oxygen","glucose"]},{"title":"Internal carotid circulation","content":[["p","The internal carotid arteries contribute major blood supply to the anterior parts of the brain. Important branches include the anterior cerebral artery and middle cerebral artery."],["p","Vascular territories overlap in useful ways, but each artery supplies characteristic regions."]],"terms":["internal carotid artery","ACA","MCA"]},{"title":"Vertebrobasilar circulation","content":[["p","The vertebral arteries join to form the basilar artery, which supplies important brainstem and cerebellar territories before contributing to posterior cerebral circulation."],["p","Posterior circulation anatomy becomes especially important when studying stroke, aneurysms, and vascular surgery."]],"terms":["vertebral artery","basilar artery","PCA"]},{"title":"Circle of Willis and venous drainage","content":[["p","The Circle of Willis is an arterial connection at the base of the brain that can provide alternative routes of flow, although its protective effect varies among people."],["p","Venous blood leaves the brain through cerebral veins and dural venous sinuses before returning to the systemic circulation."]],"terms":["Circle of Willis","dural venous sinus","venous drainage"],"quiz":{"question":"Which pair is part of the major anterior circulation of the brain?","options":["Anterior cerebral and middle cerebral arteries","Pulmonary and renal arteries","Radial and ulnar arteries","Femoral and popliteal arteries"],"correct":0,"why":"The anterior cerebral and middle cerebral arteries are major branches of the internal carotid circulation."}}]},{"id":"meninges-csf","title":"17. Meninges, CSF & Ventricles","blurb":"Study the protective layers and fluid systems around the brain and spinal cord.","level":"Applied","time":"24 min","sections":[{"title":"The meninges","content":[["p","Three connective tissue layers surround the brain and spinal cord: dura mater, arachnoid mater, and pia mater."],["p","The layers help protect neural tissue and create spaces and pathways associated with blood vessels and cerebrospinal fluid."]],"terms":["dura mater","arachnoid mater","pia mater","meninges"]},{"title":"Cerebrospinal fluid","content":[["p","Cerebrospinal fluid, or CSF, surrounds the brain and spinal cord and circulates through the ventricular and subarachnoid spaces."],["p","CSF contributes to mechanical protection and helps maintain a controlled fluid environment around the central nervous system."]],"terms":["CSF","subarachnoid space"]},{"title":"The ventricular system","content":[["p","The ventricles are connected fluid filled spaces within the brain. The lateral ventricles connect with the third ventricle, then fluid passes toward the fourth ventricle."],["p","The ventricular system connects with spaces around the brain and spinal cord through openings that allow CSF circulation."]],"terms":["lateral ventricle","third ventricle","fourth ventricle"]},{"title":"CSF production and absorption","content":[["p","Most CSF is produced by the choroid plexus. CSF is eventually returned to the bloodstream through structures associated with the arachnoid villi and granulations."],["p","Problems with CSF flow or absorption can change intracranial pressure and ventricular size."]],"terms":["choroid plexus","arachnoid granulations","intracranial pressure"],"quiz":{"question":"Which meningeal layer is closest to the surface of the brain?","options":["Pia mater","Dura mater","Arachnoid mater","Skull periosteum"],"correct":0,"why":"Pia mater closely follows the surface of the brain and spinal cord."}}]},{"id":"plasticity","title":"18. Neuroplasticity & Learning","blurb":"Discover how neural circuits change with experience, practice, and development.","level":"Applied","time":"24 min","sections":[{"title":"What is neuroplasticity?","content":[["p","Neuroplasticity describes changes in nervous system structure or function. These changes can occur with development, learning, experience, and some forms of recovery after injury."],["p","Plasticity does not mean every brain function can always be restored. It means nervous systems can change."]],"terms":["neuroplasticity","adaptation","learning"]},{"title":"Synaptic strengthening","content":[["p","When patterns of neural activity repeatedly occur together, some synapses can become stronger or weaker. Long term potentiation is one well studied example of synaptic strengthening."],["p","Synaptic changes can involve receptor trafficking, protein production, and structural changes in dendrites and synapses."]],"terms":["long term potentiation","synaptic strength","receptor trafficking"]},{"title":"Practice and motor learning","content":[["p","Repeated practice can improve performance by changing how neural circuits are recruited and refined. The brain does not merely store a movement as one file; networks adapt their activity patterns."],["p","Good practice is usually repeated, focused, and followed by feedback."]],"terms":["motor learning","practice","feedback"]},{"title":"Plasticity after injury","content":[["p","After nervous system injury, surviving networks can sometimes reorganize or strengthen alternative pathways. Rehabilitation can help guide useful changes through repeated practice."],["p","Recovery depends on the type and location of injury, the person, timing, and many other factors."]],"terms":["rehabilitation","reorganization","recovery"],"quiz":{"question":"What does neuroplasticity describe?","options":["Changes in nervous system structure or function","Only the growth of bones","A permanent lack of change in the brain","Only blood pressure changes"],"correct":0,"why":"Neuroplasticity refers to changes in nervous system structure or function."}}]},{"id":"memory-language","title":"19. Memory, Language & Cognition","blurb":"Study how networks support memory, speech, attention, and complex thought.","level":"Applied","time":"26 min","sections":[{"title":"Memory systems","content":[["p","Memory is not one single process. Different systems support working memory, episodic memories, procedural skills, and other forms of learning."],["p","The hippocampus is especially important for forming certain new declarative memories, while other brain systems support storage and retrieval over time."]],"terms":["working memory","episodic memory","procedural memory","hippocampus"]},{"title":"Language networks","content":[["p","Language depends on distributed networks involving frontal, temporal, parietal, and subcortical regions. Broca related regions contribute to language production, while posterior temporal regions are important for language comprehension."],["p","The exact organization varies across individuals, so modern neuroscience thinks in terms of networks rather than one isolated language switch."]],"terms":["language network","Broca region","temporal lobe"]},{"title":"Attention and executive function","content":[["p","Attention helps select information for deeper processing. Executive functions include planning, working memory, flexible rule use, and inhibition."],["p","Frontal and parietal networks are important for these functions, but they depend on communication across broader circuits."]],"terms":["attention","executive function","inhibition"]},{"title":"Learning from mistakes","content":[["p","Learning changes when the brain receives useful feedback. Correcting an incorrect response can strengthen the distinction between what works and what does not."],["p","For your studies, retrieving an answer from memory is often more powerful than rereading the same paragraph many times."]],"terms":["retrieval practice","feedback","learning"],"quiz":{"question":"Which structure is especially important for forming many new declarative memories?","options":["Hippocampus","Medulla","Optic chiasm","Patella"],"correct":0,"why":"The hippocampus is important for forming many new declarative memories."}}]},{"id":"sleep-consciousness","title":"20. Sleep, Arousal & Consciousness","blurb":"Explore sleep stages, wakefulness, awareness, and the brain systems that support consciousness.","level":"Applied","time":"26 min","sections":[{"title":"Why sleep matters","content":[["p","Sleep is a biological state with organized changes in brain activity and body physiology. It supports memory processing, regulation, and restoration."],["p","Sleep is not simply the brain turning off. Different sleep stages have different patterns of brain activity."]],"terms":["sleep","circadian rhythm","sleep stage"]},{"title":"Arousal systems","content":[["p","Wakefulness depends on networks in the brainstem, hypothalamus, basal forebrain, and other regions that regulate arousal and alertness."],["p","These systems interact with the cerebral cortex to support a waking brain that can process information."]],"terms":["arousal","reticular formation","hypothalamus"]},{"title":"Wakefulness versus awareness","content":[["p","Wakefulness refers broadly to being in a state capable of interacting with the environment. Awareness refers to having conscious experience or knowledge of oneself and surroundings."],["p","Neurological disorders can affect these dimensions differently, which is why clinicians use careful definitions."]],"terms":["wakefulness","awareness","consciousness"]},{"title":"Coma and disorders of consciousness","content":[["p","Coma is a state of profound unconsciousness with no normal wakefulness. Other disorders of consciousness can involve wakefulness without clear evidence of awareness."],["p","These conditions are medically distinct from brain death, which involves irreversible cessation of all brain function including the brainstem under accepted medical criteria."]],"terms":["coma","disorder of consciousness","brain death"],"quiz":{"question":"Which statement best describes brain death?","options":["Irreversible cessation of all brain function including the brainstem","A normal stage of sleep","Temporary confusion after studying","Only loss of memory"],"correct":0,"why":"Brain death refers to irreversible cessation of all brain function, including the brainstem, under accepted medical criteria."}}]},{"id":"bbb","title":"21. Blood Brain Barrier & Neuroprotection","blurb":"Learn how the brain controls its internal environment and protects sensitive tissue.","level":"Applied","time":"22 min","sections":[{"title":"The blood brain barrier","content":[["p","The blood brain barrier is a specialized interface that limits movement of many substances from blood into brain tissue. Tight junctions between brain capillary endothelial cells are important to this barrier."],["p","The barrier helps keep the neural environment stable, but it also creates challenges for delivering some medicines into the brain."]],"terms":["blood brain barrier","tight junction","endothelium"]},{"title":"Astrocytes and barrier support","content":[["p","Astrocyte processes surround blood vessels and contribute to the environment around brain capillaries. The barrier itself depends on several cell types and molecular features."],["p","Barrier function can change during inflammation, infection, tumors, and injury."]],"terms":["astrocyte endfeet","barrier function","inflammation"]},{"title":"Neuroinflammation","content":[["p","Immune signaling in the nervous system can be helpful during infection or injury, but excessive or persistent inflammation can damage tissue."],["p","Microglia and other cells participate in these responses. Modern neuroscience studies both protective and harmful effects."]],"terms":["microglia","neuroinflammation","immune signaling"]},{"title":"Why protection matters","content":[["p","The skull, meninges, CSF, blood brain barrier, and local cellular systems all contribute different forms of protection."],["p","No single layer is a perfect shield. The nervous system relies on multiple overlapping defenses."]],"terms":["skull","meninges","CSF","neuroprotection"],"quiz":{"question":"What is one important function of the blood brain barrier?","options":["Help control which substances enter brain tissue from blood","Make every neuron fire continuously","Store all long term memories","Control eye color"],"correct":0,"why":"The blood brain barrier helps regulate movement of substances between blood and brain tissue."}}]},{"id":"exam","title":"22. Neurological Examination","blurb":"Learn how clinicians use observation, movement, sensation, reflexes, and cranial nerves to localize problems.","level":"Clinical Foundation","time":"28 min","sections":[{"title":"The neurological exam","content":[["p","A neurological examination tests several systems: mental status, cranial nerves, motor function, sensation, coordination, reflexes, and gait."],["p","Each part gives clues about which nervous system structures are functioning and how they connect."]],"terms":["neurological exam","mental status","motor","sensory"]},{"title":"Strength and tone","content":[["p","Clinicians can assess muscle strength, tone, and the pattern of weakness. Patterns can help distinguish problems involving upper motor neurons, lower motor neurons, nerves, muscles, or neuromuscular junctions."],["p","This is a localization skill built on anatomy rather than a list of isolated findings."]],"terms":["strength","tone","motor neuron","localization"]},{"title":"Sensation and reflexes","content":[["p","Sensation can be tested for different modalities such as light touch, pain, temperature, vibration, and position sense."],["p","Reflexes give information about sensory input, spinal circuits, motor output, and descending control from the brain."]],"terms":["sensation","reflex","proprioception"]},{"title":"Cranial nerve examination","content":[["p","Examining eye movements, facial movement, hearing, swallowing, tongue movement, smell, and vision can reveal problems in cranial nerves or their connections."],["p","One abnormal finding rarely tells the whole story. Clinicians combine findings into a pattern."]],"terms":["cranial nerve","eye movement","facial nerve"],"quiz":{"question":"What is neurological localization?","options":["Using patterns of findings to estimate where a nervous system problem is located","Choosing a diagnosis from one symptom alone","A type of brain scan","A surgical instrument"],"correct":0,"why":"Neurological localization uses examination findings and anatomy to estimate where a problem may be located."}}]},{"id":"disorders","title":"23. Neurological Disorders","blurb":"Survey major neurological disorders and learn how anatomy connects to symptoms.","level":"Clinical","time":"30 min","sections":[{"title":"Stroke","content":[["p","A stroke occurs when a brain region is injured because its blood supply is blocked or because a blood vessel bleeds. Symptoms depend on the affected location and network."],["p","Because brain functions are organized in pathways, the pattern of symptoms can provide clues about the site of injury."]],"terms":["stroke","ischemia","hemorrhage"]},{"title":"Seizures and epilepsy","content":[["p","A seizure is a period of abnormal, excessive, or synchronized neuronal activity that can produce changes in movement, sensation, behavior, awareness, or other functions."],["p","Epilepsy is a neurological disorder characterized by a tendency to have recurrent unprovoked seizures. Seizures are not all the same."]],"terms":["seizure","epilepsy","abnormal electrical activity"]},{"title":"Tumors and mass effect","content":[["p","Brain tumors are abnormal growths of cells in or around the nervous system. Their effects depend on their location, type, growth pattern, and relationship to nearby structures."],["p","A growing lesion can affect function by invading tissue, compressing structures, or changing fluid dynamics."]],"terms":["brain tumor","mass effect","neoplasm"]},{"title":"Neurodegenerative disease","content":[["p","Neurodegenerative disorders involve progressive dysfunction or loss of neurons and neural networks. Different diseases preferentially affect different systems."],["p","Examples include Parkinson disease and Alzheimer disease. Learning the affected circuits is more useful than memorizing names alone."]],"terms":["neurodegeneration","Parkinson disease","Alzheimer disease"],"quiz":{"question":"Why can the symptoms of a stroke help localize the injured brain area?","options":["Different brain regions and pathways support different functions","Every stroke affects the whole body equally","Symptoms are unrelated to anatomy","The skull determines all symptoms"],"correct":0,"why":"Different brain regions and pathways support different functions, so symptom patterns can provide localization clues."}}]},{"id":"imaging","title":"24. Neuroimaging & Clinical Maps","blurb":"Learn CT, MRI, vascular imaging, and how images connect to anatomy.","level":"Clinical","time":"28 min","sections":[{"title":"CT scanning","content":[["p","Computed tomography uses x rays and computer processing to create cross sectional images. It is fast and widely used in urgent neurological settings."],["p","CT can show bleeding, fractures, swelling, and other abnormalities, while its soft tissue contrast differs from MRI."]],"terms":["CT","computed tomography","x ray"]},{"title":"MRI","content":[["p","Magnetic resonance imaging uses magnetic fields and radiofrequency energy to produce detailed images of tissue. Different sequences emphasize different tissue properties."],["p","MRI is especially useful for many brain and spinal cord questions because it provides strong soft tissue contrast."]],"terms":["MRI","magnetic resonance imaging","sequence"]},{"title":"Angiography","content":[["p","Angiography creates images of blood vessels. It can help identify narrowing, aneurysms, arteriovenous malformations, and other vascular conditions."],["p","Different methods include CT angiography, MR angiography, and catheter based digital subtraction angiography."]],"terms":["angiography","CTA","MRA","DSA"]},{"title":"Reading an image as anatomy","content":[["p","Before interpreting an abnormality, identify normal landmarks. Ask which plane you are viewing, what structures should be present, and how the structures relate to each other."],["p","Good imaging interpretation combines anatomy with the clinical story instead of treating the picture as a standalone answer."]],"terms":["anatomical landmark","imaging plane","clinical correlation"],"quiz":{"question":"Which imaging modality is especially known for detailed soft tissue imaging of the brain?","options":["MRI","Thermometer","Electrocardiogram","Dental x ray only"],"correct":0,"why":"MRI provides excellent soft tissue contrast and is widely used for brain imaging."}}]},{"id":"surgery","title":"25. Neurosurgery Foundations","blurb":"Connect anatomy, clinical reasoning, safety, technology, teamwork, and the long path toward neurosurgery.","level":"Neurosurgery Foundation","time":"32 min","sections":[{"title":"What neurosurgeons treat","content":[["p","Neurosurgeons treat selected conditions involving the brain, spine, peripheral nerves, and related structures. Examples include certain tumors, vascular conditions, trauma, and spinal disorders."],["p","Not every neurological problem needs surgery. A major part of surgical reasoning is deciding when surgery is useful and when another treatment is better."]],"terms":["neurosurgery","brain surgery","spine surgery"]},{"title":"Surgical planning","content":[["p","Surgical planning starts with diagnosis and anatomy. Surgeons consider symptoms, imaging, risks, goals, available treatments, and the relationships between the target and nearby important structures."],["p","Technology such as image guidance and specialized monitoring can support planning, but technology does not replace anatomical understanding."]],"terms":["surgical planning","neuronavigation","intraoperative monitoring"]},{"title":"The operating room team","content":[["p","Neurosurgery is a team activity. Surgeons work with anesthesiologists, nurses, radiologists, neurologists, technicians, therapists, and many other professionals."],["p","Safe care depends on communication, checklists, preparation, and knowing when to ask another expert for help."]],"terms":["operating room","anesthesiology","teamwork","checklist"]},{"title":"The path to becoming a neurosurgeon","content":[["p","In the United States, the path generally includes secondary school, college, medical school, and residency training. Neurosurgery residency is typically seven years, with some surgeons completing additional fellowship training."],["p","Right now, the most useful preparation is broad: biology, chemistry, physics, mathematics, writing, communication, curiosity, and the habit of learning carefully."]],"terms":["medical school","residency","fellowship","career path"],"quiz":{"question":"What is a strong first step in neurosurgical planning?","options":["Build a careful diagnosis and anatomical map before choosing treatment","Skip the examination","Start operating immediately","Ignore imaging"],"correct":0,"why":"Safe surgical planning begins with careful evaluation, diagnosis, anatomy, goals, risks, and treatment options."}}]}];

const glossaryTerms = [
  ['Action potential','A rapid, characteristic electrical signal in an excitable cell.'],['Axon','A neuronal process that commonly carries signals away from the cell body.'],['Brainstem','The midbrain, pons, and medulla; contains pathways and important neural circuits.'],['Cerebellum','A brain structure involved in coordination, balance, timing, and motor learning.'],['Cerebrum','The largest major division of the brain, involved in many higher level functions.'],['Dendrite','A neuronal process that commonly receives incoming signals.'],['Glia','Support cells of nervous tissue with many specialized functions.'],['Hippocampus','A structure important for memory formation and related learning processes.'],['Meninges','Protective connective tissue layers around the brain and spinal cord.'],['Myelin','An insulating layer around many axons.'],['Neuron','An electrically active cell specialized to receive and send information.'],['Neuroplasticity','The nervous system’s ability to change structure or function with experience and other conditions.'],['Neurotransmitter','A chemical signal released by neurons to communicate across synapses.'],['Refractory period','A period after an action potential when another action potential is harder or temporarily impossible to produce normally.'],['Synapse','A communication junction between a neuron and another cell.'],['Thalamus','A major relay and processing region for many sensory and motor signals.'],['Threshold','A membrane voltage level that can trigger an action potential under the relevant conditions.'],['Ventricle','A fluid filled space within the brain that is part of the ventricular system.']
];

const stages = [{"name":"1. Biology for Neuroscience","courseId":"biology"},{"name":"2. Cells, Tissues & Organelles","courseId":"cells"},{"name":"3. Anatomy Language","courseId":"anatomy-language"},{"name":"4. Neuron Anatomy","courseId":"neuron-anatomy"},{"name":"5. Glia & Myelin","courseId":"glia"},{"name":"6. Resting Membrane Potential","courseId":"resting"},{"name":"7. Action Potentials","courseId":"action"},{"name":"8. Synapses & Neurotransmitters","courseId":"synapses"},{"name":"9. Spinal Cord & Reflexes","courseId":"spinal"},{"name":"10. Cranial Nerves","courseId":"cranial"},{"name":"11. Major Brain Anatomy","courseId":"brain-anatomy"},{"name":"12. Cerebral Cortex","courseId":"cortex"},{"name":"13. Deep Brain Structures","courseId":"deep"},{"name":"14. Brainstem & Cerebellum","courseId":"brainstem-cerebellum"},{"name":"15. Sensory & Motor Pathways","courseId":"pathways"},{"name":"16. Brain Blood Supply","courseId":"blood"},{"name":"17. Meninges, CSF & Ventricles","courseId":"meninges-csf"},{"name":"18. Neuroplasticity & Learning","courseId":"plasticity"},{"name":"19. Memory, Language & Cognition","courseId":"memory-language"},{"name":"20. Sleep, Arousal & Consciousness","courseId":"sleep-consciousness"},{"name":"21. Blood Brain Barrier & Neuroprotection","courseId":"bbb"},{"name":"22. Neurological Examination","courseId":"exam"},{"name":"23. Neurological Disorders","courseId":"disorders"},{"name":"24. Neuroimaging & Clinical Maps","courseId":"imaging"},{"name":"25. Neurosurgery Foundations","courseId":"surgery"}];

const stageByCourse = Object.fromEntries(stages.map((s,i)=>[s.courseId,i]));
const curriculumList = document.getElementById('curriculumList');
const continueBtn = document.getElementById('continueBtn');
const openNextBtn = document.getElementById('openNextBtn');
const courseDialog = document.getElementById('courseDialog');
const closeDialog = document.getElementById('closeDialog');
const chapterNav = document.getElementById('chapterNav');
const readerTitle = document.getElementById('readerTitle');
const readerContent = document.getElementById('readerContent');
const readerCallout = document.getElementById('readerCallout');
const readerTerms = document.getElementById('readerTerms');
const readerQuiz = document.getElementById('readerQuiz');
const readerSectionCount = document.getElementById('readerSectionCount');
const chapterProgressBar = document.getElementById('chapterProgressBar');
const nextSectionBtn = document.getElementById('nextSection');
const prevSectionBtn = document.getElementById('prevSection');
const toast = document.getElementById('toast');

let activeCourseIndex = 0;
let activeSectionIndex = 0;
let activeQuizAnswered = false;
let quizTimer = null;

function save(){ localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(state)); }

function showToast(message){
  toast.textContent = message;
  toast.classList.add('show');
  clearTimeout(showToast.timer);
  showToast.timer = setTimeout(()=>toast.classList.remove('show'),2600);
}

function currentStageIndex(){ return Math.min(state.unlocked, stages.length - 1); }
function courseForStage(index){ return courses[stageByCourse[stages[index].courseId]]; }
function isStageComplete(index){ return !!state.completed[stages[index].courseId]; }
function quizBest(index){ return Number(state.quizBest[stages[index].courseId] || 0); }

function updateDashboard(){
  const completedCount = stages.filter((_,i)=>isStageComplete(i)).length;
  const percent = Math.round((completedCount / stages.length) * 100);
  document.getElementById('progressText').textContent = `${percent}%`;
  document.getElementById('xpText').textContent = `${state.xp} XP`;
  document.getElementById('stageProgress').textContent = `${completedCount} / ${stages.length}`;
  const current = currentStageIndex();
  document.getElementById('currentStageName').textContent = stages[current].name;
  const bests = stages.map((_,i)=>quizBest(i));
  document.getElementById('quizBestText').textContent = bests.length ? `${Math.max(...bests)}%` : '0%';
  document.getElementById('badgeText').textContent = `${state.badges.length}`;
  const ring = document.querySelector('.dash-ring');
  if(ring) ring.style.setProperty('--ring', `${percent}%`);
  const next = currentStageIndex();
  const course = courseForStage(next);
  document.getElementById('nextLessonTitle').textContent = `Chapter ${String(next+1).padStart(2,'0')}: ${course.title}`;
  document.getElementById('nextLessonBlurb').textContent = course.blurb;
  const isDone = isStageComplete(next);
  document.getElementById('openNextBtn').textContent = isDone ? (next === stages.length - 1 ? 'Review capstone' : 'Review chapter') : 'Open chapter';
  renderCurriculum();
}

function renderCurriculum(){
  curriculumList.innerHTML='';
  stages.forEach((stage,i)=>{
    const unlocked = i <= state.unlocked;
    const complete = isStageComplete(i);
    const current = i === currentStageIndex();
    const card = document.createElement('article');
    card.className = `curriculum-card ${!unlocked?'locked':''} ${current?'current':''}`;
    card.innerHTML = `<div class="stage-index">${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</div>
      <div class="stage-main"><h3>${stage.name}</h3><p>${courses[stageByCourse[stage.courseId]].blurb}</p><div class="stage-meta"><span class="stage-tag">${courses[stageByCourse[stage.courseId]].level}</span><span class="stage-tag">${courses[stageByCourse[stage.courseId]].time}</span></div></div>
      <div class="stage-status ${complete?'done':''} ${current&&!complete?'current':''}">${complete?'✓ Complete':unlocked?(current?'Current':'Unlocked'):'🔒 Locked'}</div>`;
    if(unlocked){
      const button=document.createElement('button');
      button.className='button secondary';
      button.textContent=complete?'Review':'Study';
      button.addEventListener('click',()=>openCourse(i));
      card.appendChild(button);
    }
    curriculumList.appendChild(card);
  });
}

function openCourse(index){
  if(index>state.unlocked){ showToast('Finish the earlier chapter first. One brain map at a time. 🧠'); return; }
  activeCourseIndex=index;
  activeSectionIndex=0;
  activeQuizAnswered=false;
  state.lastOpened=index;
  save();
  buildChapterNav();
  renderSection();
  document.body.classList.add('lesson-open');
  if(typeof courseDialog.showModal==='function'){
    if(!courseDialog.open) courseDialog.showModal();
  } else {
    courseDialog.setAttribute('open','');
  }
}

function closeCourse(){
  clearTimeout(quizTimer);
  document.body.classList.remove('lesson-open');
  if(courseDialog.open && typeof courseDialog.close==='function') courseDialog.close();
  else courseDialog.removeAttribute('open');
}
closeDialog.addEventListener('click',closeCourse);
courseDialog.addEventListener('click',(event)=>{ if(event.target===courseDialog) closeCourse(); });
document.addEventListener('keydown',(event)=>{
  if(event.key==='Escape' && courseDialog.open) closeCourse();
});

function buildChapterNav(){
  const course=courseForStage(activeCourseIndex);
  document.getElementById('courseKicker').textContent=`COURSE ${String(activeCourseIndex+1).padStart(2,'0')} • 100 LESSONS • ${course.level.toUpperCase()}`;
  document.getElementById('courseDialogTitle').textContent=course.title;
  chapterNav.innerHTML='';
  course.sections.forEach((section,i)=>{
    const b=document.createElement('button');
    b.className='toc-btn';
    b.innerHTML=`<span class="toc-num">${i+1}</span><span>${section.title}</span>`;
    b.addEventListener('click',()=>{
      activeSectionIndex=i;
      activeQuizAnswered=false;
      renderSection();
    });
    chapterNav.appendChild(b);
  });
}

function renderContentBlocks(blocks){
  readerContent.innerHTML='';
  blocks.forEach(block=>{
    const type=block[0];
    if(type==='p'){
      const p=document.createElement('p'); p.textContent=block[1]; readerContent.appendChild(p);
    } else if(type==='h4'){
      const h=document.createElement('h4'); h.textContent=block[1]; readerContent.appendChild(h);
    } else if(type==='ul'){
      const ul=document.createElement('ul');
      block[1].forEach(item=>{const li=document.createElement('li');li.textContent=item;ul.appendChild(li)});
      readerContent.appendChild(ul);
    } else if(type==='diagram'){
      const wrap=document.createElement('div'); wrap.className='reader-diagram';
      block[1].forEach(pair=>{const box=document.createElement('div');box.className='diagram-box';box.innerHTML=`<strong>${pair[0]}</strong><span>${pair[1]}</span>`;wrap.appendChild(box)});
      readerContent.appendChild(wrap);
    }
  });
}

function renderQuiz(quiz){
  readerQuiz.innerHTML='';
  if(!quiz){ readerQuiz.classList.add('hidden'); return; }
  readerQuiz.classList.remove('hidden');
  const title=document.createElement('h4'); title.textContent='🧪 Checkpoint quiz'; readerQuiz.appendChild(title);
  const question=document.createElement('div'); question.className='quiz-question'; question.textContent=quiz.question; readerQuiz.appendChild(question);
  const answers=document.createElement('div'); answers.className='quiz-answers'; readerQuiz.appendChild(answers);
  const feedback=document.createElement('div'); feedback.className='quiz-feedback'; feedback.id='activeQuizFeedback'; readerQuiz.appendChild(feedback);
  const score=document.createElement('div'); score.className='quiz-score'; score.textContent='Checkpoint: answer correctly to clear this quiz. Wrong answers can be retried.'; readerQuiz.appendChild(score);
  quiz.options.forEach((option,index)=>{
    const btn=document.createElement('button'); btn.className='quiz-option'; btn.textContent=option;
    btn.addEventListener('click',()=>answerQuiz(index,btn,quiz,answers,feedback));
    answers.appendChild(btn);
  });
}

function answerQuiz(index,clicked,quiz,answers,feedback){
  if(activeQuizAnswered) return;
  [...answers.children].forEach(button=>button.classList.remove('incorrect','correct'));
  if(index===quiz.correct){
    activeQuizAnswered=true;
    [...answers.children].forEach((button,i)=>{button.disabled=true;if(i===quiz.correct)button.classList.add('correct')});
    feedback.innerHTML=`<strong>Correct.</strong> ${quiz.why}`;
    const course=courseForStage(activeCourseIndex);
    const quizKey=`${course.id}:${activeSectionIndex}`;
    const firstClear=!state.quizCleared[quizKey];
    if(firstClear){ state.quizCleared[quizKey]=true; state.xp += 25; save(); updateDashboard(); showToast('+25 XP; checkpoint cleared! ⚡'); }
    if(activeSectionIndex===course.sections.length-1) maybeUnlockCurrentCourse();
  } else {
    clicked.classList.add('incorrect');
    feedback.innerHTML=`<strong>Not quite.</strong> ${quiz.why} Try again. 🧠`;
  }
}

function maybeUnlockCurrentCourse(){
  const course=courseForStage(activeCourseIndex);
  const stage=stages[activeCourseIndex];
  if(state.completed[course.id]) return;
  state.quizBest[course.id]=100;
  state.completed[course.id]=true;
  if(activeCourseIndex>=state.unlocked && activeCourseIndex<stages.length-1) state.unlocked=activeCourseIndex+1;
  if(!state.badges.includes(course.id)) state.badges.push(course.id);
  save();
  updateDashboard();
  showToast(`Course ${activeCourseIndex+1} complete! The next course is unlocked. 🎓`);
  if(activeCourseIndex < stages.length-1){
    nextSectionBtn.textContent='Opening next course…';
    clearTimeout(quizTimer);
    quizTimer=setTimeout(()=>{
      const next=activeCourseIndex+1;
      closeCourse();
      setTimeout(()=>openCourse(next),220);
    },1200);
  } else {
    nextSectionBtn.textContent='Journey complete 🎉';
  }
}

function renderSection(){
  const course=courseForStage(activeCourseIndex);
  const section=course.sections[activeSectionIndex];
  document.getElementById('readerType').textContent=course.lab && activeSectionIndex===0?'TEXTBOOK + LAB':'TEXTBOOK CHAPTER';
  const globalLesson = stages.slice(0,activeCourseIndex).reduce((sum,s)=>sum+courseForStage(stageByCourse[s.courseId]).sections.length,0)+activeSectionIndex+1;
  readerSectionCount.textContent=`Lesson ${globalLesson} of 100 • Course ${activeCourseIndex+1} of ${stages.length} • Section ${activeSectionIndex+1} of ${course.sections.length}`;
  readerTitle.textContent=section.title;
  renderContentBlocks(section.content);
  if(section.callout){ readerCallout.className=`reader-callout ${section.quiz?'warning':''}`; readerCallout.textContent=section.callout; readerCallout.classList.remove('hidden'); } else { readerCallout.classList.add('hidden'); }
  readerTerms.innerHTML='';
  (section.terms||[]).forEach(term=>{const span=document.createElement('span');span.className='reader-term';span.textContent=term;readerTerms.appendChild(span)});
  activeQuizAnswered=false;
  renderQuiz(section.quiz);
  chapterNav.querySelectorAll('.toc-btn').forEach((b,i)=>{b.classList.toggle('active',i===activeSectionIndex);b.classList.toggle('done',i<activeSectionIndex||isStageComplete(activeCourseIndex))});
  prevSectionBtn.disabled=activeSectionIndex===0;
  nextSectionBtn.textContent=activeSectionIndex===course.sections.length-1?(isStageComplete(activeCourseIndex)?(activeCourseIndex<stages.length-1?'Next stage →':'Finish'):(section.quiz?'Complete checkpoint':'Finish chapter')):'Next section';
  chapterProgressBar.style.width=`${((activeSectionIndex+1)/course.sections.length)*100}%`;
  if(course.id==='action' && activeSectionIndex===0) injectLabMiniPrompt();
}

function injectLabMiniPrompt(){
  const note=document.createElement('div');
  note.className='reader-callout';
  note.textContent='⚡ After this section, jump to the Action Potential Lab below the textbook. Drag the slider and explain each stage in your own words.';
  readerContent.appendChild(note);
}

function nextSection(){
  const course=courseForStage(activeCourseIndex);
  const section=course.sections[activeSectionIndex];
  if(activeSectionIndex < course.sections.length-1){
    activeSectionIndex++;
    renderSection();
    return;
  }
  if(section.quiz && !activeQuizAnswered){
    showToast('Take the checkpoint quiz before moving on. 🧪');
    return;
  }
  if(!section.quiz && !isStageComplete(activeCourseIndex)){
    state.completed[course.id]=true;
    state.quizBest[course.id]=0;
    state.xp += 10;
    if(activeCourseIndex===state.unlocked && activeCourseIndex<stages.length-1) state.unlocked=activeCourseIndex+1;
    if(!state.badges.includes(course.id)) state.badges.push(course.id);
    save(); updateDashboard();
    showToast(`Course ${activeCourseIndex+1} complete. +10 XP 📖`);
    if(activeCourseIndex<stages.length-1){
      clearTimeout(quizTimer);
      quizTimer=setTimeout(()=>{closeCourse();setTimeout(()=>openCourse(activeCourseIndex+1),220)},1200);
    }
    return;
  }
  if(activeCourseIndex < stages.length-1){ closeCourse(); setTimeout(()=>openCourse(activeCourseIndex+1),180); }
}

prevSectionBtn.addEventListener('click',()=>{if(activeSectionIndex>0){activeSectionIndex--;renderSection()}});
nextSectionBtn.addEventListener('click',nextSection);
continueBtn.addEventListener('click',()=>openCourse(currentStageIndex()));
openNextBtn.addEventListener('click',()=>openCourse(currentStageIndex()));

document.getElementById('resetProgress').addEventListener('click',()=>{
  if(!confirm('Reset all NeuroPath progress?')) return;
  state={...defaultState,completed:{},quizBest:{},quizCleared:{},badges:[]};
  save(); updateDashboard(); showToast('Progress reset. Fresh notebook. 🧠');
});

const stagesLab=[
  {title:'Resting potential',voltage:'−70 mV',description:'The neuron is at rest and maintains ion gradients across its membrane.',ion:'Think: stable gradients + selective permeability',prompt:'Why is the membrane voltage not zero at rest?'},
  {title:'Threshold',voltage:'about −55 mV',description:'A sufficient voltage change can bring the membrane to threshold and trigger a regenerative action potential.',ion:'Think: threshold → many voltage gated Na⁺ channels open',prompt:'Why does reaching threshold matter?'},
  {title:'Depolarization',voltage:'rising toward +30 mV',description:'Sodium ions move inward through open voltage gated sodium channels, making the membrane rapidly more positive.',ion:'Think: Na⁺ influx dominates the rising phase',prompt:'Which ion is especially important during the rising phase?'},
  {title:'Peak',voltage:'about +30 mV',description:'The membrane reaches a positive peak as sodium channels inactivate and potassium conductance becomes important.',ion:'Think: Na⁺ channels inactivate + K⁺ channels activate',prompt:'Why does the voltage stop rising?'},
  {title:'Repolarization',voltage:'falling toward rest',description:'Potassium ions move outward through open potassium channels, helping return the membrane toward negative values.',ion:'Think: K⁺ efflux helps bring voltage down',prompt:'Which ion movement helps the falling phase?'},
  {title:'Hyperpolarization',voltage:'below resting level',description:'Potassium conductance can remain elevated briefly, making the membrane more negative than resting level.',ion:'Think: a temporary overshoot below rest',prompt:'Why can the membrane become more negative than rest?'},
  {title:'Recovery',voltage:'returning toward rest',description:'Channel states and ion gradients settle back toward the conditions that support another signal.',ion:'Think: channels reset + gradients are maintained',prompt:'What has to reset before another signal can occur normally?'}
];
const slider=document.getElementById('potentialSlider');
const stageButtons=document.getElementById('stageButtons');
stagesLab.forEach((stage,index)=>{const b=document.createElement('button');b.textContent=stage.title;b.addEventListener('click',()=>{slider.value=index;renderLabStage(index)});stageButtons.appendChild(b)});
function renderLabStage(index){const s=stagesLab[index];document.getElementById('voltageLabel').textContent=s.voltage;document.getElementById('stageTitle').textContent=s.title;document.getElementById('stageDescription').textContent=s.description;document.getElementById('ionBox').textContent=s.ion;document.getElementById('teachPrompt').textContent=s.prompt;stageButtons.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((b,i)=>b.classList.toggle('active',i===index))}
slider.addEventListener('input',()=>renderLabStage(Number(slider.value)));renderLabStage(0);

function renderGlossary(query=''){
  const host=document.getElementById('glossary');
  const q=query.trim().toLowerCase();host.innerHTML='';
  glossaryTerms.filter(([term,def])=>!q||`${term} ${def}`.toLowerCase().includes(q)).forEach(([term,def])=>{const item=document.createElement('article');item.className='glossary-item';const h=document.createElement('h3');h.textContent=term;const p=document.createElement('p');p.textContent=def;item.append(h,p);host.appendChild(item)});
}
document.getElementById('glossarySearch').addEventListener('input',(e)=>renderGlossary(e.target.value));
renderGlossary();
updateDashboard();
